13994:
For the curved liquid surface (meniscus) forming an angle \theta(x) with the horizontal:
The slope of the surface is given by:
\tan\theta(x) \approx \frac{dy}{dx}
The curvature \frac{1}{R} of the surface for small slope (\theta \ll 1) is:
\frac{1}{R} \approx \frac{d^2y}{dx^2}
The excess Laplace pressure jump across the curved liquid-air interface is:
\Delta P = \frac{S}{R} = S \frac{d^2y}{dx^2}
At any point at height y above the flat horizontal liquid level (y = 0), this Laplace pressure difference must balance the hydrostatic pressure of the elevated liquid column:
\Delta P = \rho g y
Equating the two expressions:
S \frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = \rho g y \implies \frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = \frac{\rho g y}{S}

Thus, the correct differential equation is \frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = \frac{\rho g y}{S}.
→ (B)

13995:
