14039:
Using the Rydberg formula for spectral absorption in hydrogen atom (Z = 1):
\frac{1}{\lambda} = R_H\left(\frac{1}{n_1^2} - \frac{1}{n_2^2}\right)
For transition n = 2 \rightarrow n = 3:
\frac{1}{\lambda_1} = R_H\left(\frac{1}{2^2} - \frac{1}{3^2}\right) = R_H\left(\frac{1}{4} - \frac{1}{9}\right) = R_H\left(\frac{5}{36}\right) \implies \lambda_1 = \frac{36}{5 R_H}
For transition n = 4 \rightarrow n = 6:
\frac{1}{\lambda_2} = R_H\left(\frac{1}{4^2} - \frac{1}{6^2}\right) = R_H\left(\frac{1}{16} - \frac{1}{36}\right) = R_H\left(\frac{9 - 4}{144}\right) = R_H\left(\frac{5}{144}\right) \implies \lambda_2 = \frac{144}{5 R_H}
Taking the ratio of wavelengths:
\frac{\lambda_1}{\lambda_2} = \frac{36 / (5 R_H)}{144 / (5 R_H)} = \frac{36}{144} = \frac{1}{4}

Therefore, the ratio of wavelengths is \frac{1}{4}.
→ (D)

14043:
For hydrogen-like ions in the first Bohr orbit (n = 1):
E_n = -R_H \frac{Z^2}{n^2}, \quad r_n = a_0 \frac{n^2}{Z}
Given R_H = 2.18 \times 10^{-18}\text{ J} and a_0 = 52.9\text{ pm}:
