14052:
Phosphoric acid (\text{H}_3\text{PO}_4) is a triprotic acid that ionizes in three stepwise equilibria:
\text{H}_3\text{PO}_4 \rightleftharpoons \text{H}^{+} + \text{H}_2\text{PO}_4^-, \quad K_{a1}
\text{H}_2\text{PO}_4^- \rightleftharpoons \text{H}^{+} + \text{HPO}_4^{2-}, \quad K_{a2}
\text{HPO}_4^{2-} \rightleftharpoons \text{H}^{+} + \text{PO}_4^{3-}, \quad K_{a3}
Statement A: The overall ionization reaction is the sum of the three steps, so K = K_{a1} \cdot K_{a2} \cdot K_{a3}, which gives \log K = \log K_{a1} + \log K_{a2} + \log K_{a3} (True).
Statement B: It is much easier to remove a proton from a neutral molecule (\text{H}_3\text{PO}_4) than from negatively charged ions (\text{H}_2\text{PO}_4^- and \text{HPO}_4^{2-}). Hence, \text{H}_3\text{PO}_4 is a stronger acid (True).
Statement C: Due to increasing electrostatic attraction on the proton, the stepwise ionization constants decrease sharply: K_{a1} > K_{a2} > K_{a3} (True).
Statement D: K_{a1} = K_{a3} + 2K_{a2} is an arbitrary incorrect formula.

Therefore, statements A, B, and C only are true.
→ (D)

14066:
For the gas phase equilibrium \text{A}(g) \rightleftharpoons 2\text{B}(g):
The equilibrium constant in terms of concentration is:
K_c = \frac{k_f}{k_b}
Given that the backward reaction rate constant k_b is higher than the forward rate constant k_f by a factor of 2500:
\frac{k_b}{k_f} = 2500 \implies K_c = \frac{1}{2500} = 4 \times 10^{-4}
The relationship between K_p and K_c is:
K_p = K_c (R T)^{\Delta n_g}
