14082:
The unit of the rate constant is \text{s}^{-1} (k = 0.03\text{ s}^{-1}), which identifies the reaction as first-order:
Using the integrated first-order rate law:
t = \frac{2.303}{k} \log_{10}\left(\frac{[A]_0}{[A]}\right)
Given:
[A]_0 = 7.2\text{ mol L}^{-1}, \quad [A] = 0.9\text{ mol L}^{-1}
\frac{[A]_0}{[A]} = \frac{7.2}{0.9} = 8 = 2^3
Substituting into the rate equation:
t = \frac{2.303}{0.03} \log_{10}(2^3) = \frac{2.303 \times 3 \times 0.301}{0.03}
t = \frac{2.303 \times 0.903}{0.03} = \frac{2.0796}{0.03} = 69.32\text{ s} \approx 69.3\text{ s}
Alternatively, [A]_0 undergoes 3 successive half-lives to reach 0.9\text{ mol L}^{-1}:
t_{1/2} = \frac{0.693}{0.03} = 23.1\text{ s} \implies t = 3 \times 23.1 = 69.3\text{ s}

Therefore, the time required is 69.3 s.
→ (A)
